#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711 :topic/difficulty 0.9795434122769167 :topic/id #uuid "608cf86b-83ea-5cc3-a6a8-222e7fa44e21" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064104 :question/id #uuid "fe999c48-1afd-5949-b4f9-533c09511d95" :question/math-academy-id "e-5295" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=3x^2+1$ where the tangent line has a slope of $-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3x^2+1.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to $f'(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =6x+0 \\\\  & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set $f'(x) = -1$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-1 \\\\ 6x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-1$ at the point where $x=-\\dfrac 1 6.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00a74f63-a4eb-5894-b4a4-eb4fd297a3f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Curve With a Certain Slope"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064342 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063980 :question/id #uuid "9c9b5972-f775-5cbe-8e66-2a0309f24f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-142" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^2 + \\dfrac {1}{x}$ at the point where $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^2+\\dfrac 1 x.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to $f'(2).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot x-{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =2x-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =2(2)-(2{)}^{-2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $\\dfrac{15}{4}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ffbc4bb-e179-543f-bcb5-3aaf6540a8a1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line of a Curve at a Given Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064343 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063983 :question/id #uuid "3138f267-3903-510c-86e8-5597d3689262" :question/math-academy-id "e-143" :question/problem "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^4 + 3\\sqrt [3]{x^2}$ at the point $(1,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "This time, we'll use $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {x}^{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{-1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =4(1{)}^{3}+2(1{)}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $6.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13570-d504-53e6-8e02-0f1e85047a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line of a Curve With Fractional Powers at a Given Point"}] :topic/math-academy-id 332 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :topic/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}]}
